1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,428
Here's how to reverse engineer Rocketlane from a written spec.

2
00:00:03,428 --> 00:00:07,400
Claude passed all twenty-eight checks, and found eighteen gaps in my spec.

3
00:00:08,217 --> 00:00:09,924
We gave Claude one Markdown file.

4
00:00:10,095 --> 00:00:13,785
It copies the part of Rocketlane a small team actually needs for client onboarding.

5
00:00:14,020 --> 00:00:15,983
Templates with a checklist for every stage.

6
00:00:16,324 --> 00:00:18,557
One project per client, copied from a template.

7
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:21,473
Handoffs that pass the project to a new owner, with a note.

8
00:00:21,772 --> 00:00:24,373
And a read-only link that shows the client their progress.

9
00:00:25,483 --> 00:00:27,253
The core rule is the stage gate.

10
00:00:27,445 --> 00:00:31,883
Every client moves through four stages: kickoff, setup, training and live.

11
00:00:32,139 --> 00:00:36,272
A project can only leave a stage when every task in it is done, or skipped with a reason.

12
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:39,061
Bluebird is in setup, with two tasks still open.

13
00:00:39,296 --> 00:00:42,831
So the handoff to training is refused, and the app names the open tasks.

14
00:00:43,917 --> 00:00:46,391
Claude built it in four phases, one prompt each.

15
00:00:46,605 --> 00:00:49,485
The foundation, with people, templates and sign-in.

16
00:00:49,719 --> 00:00:51,874
Projects, checklists and stage gates.

17
00:00:52,109 --> 00:00:55,239
Notifications, the daily digest, and projects on hold.

18
00:00:55,425 --> 00:00:58,326
Then the client link, handoffs and CSV export.

19
00:00:58,667 --> 00:01:03,227
After every phase, the acceptance checks ran in a real browser, from fresh seed data.

20
00:01:04,317 --> 00:01:05,213
Here's what it built.

21
00:01:05,512 --> 00:01:10,120
The board shows every client by stage, with three in progress and four overdue tasks.

22
00:01:10,376 --> 00:01:14,831
Bluebird's project page shows the setup checklist, with training locked until setup is done.

23
00:01:15,020 --> 00:01:17,900
A new project previews all six tasks and their due dates.

24
00:01:18,092 --> 00:01:20,460
Each morning, a digest lists what's overdue.

25
00:01:20,609 --> 00:01:24,308
And the client's link shows only shared tasks, never internal ones.

26
00:01:25,383 --> 00:01:32,187
In one final run, on a fresh database, all twenty-eight checks passed, along with one hundred and nineteen automated tests.

27
00:01:33,283 --> 00:01:35,267
But the more useful result is what broke.

28
00:01:35,545 --> 00:01:37,827
The build exposed eighteen gaps in the spec.

29
00:01:38,105 --> 00:01:39,641
Places where Claude had to guess.

30
00:01:39,854 --> 00:01:43,730
A live project still counted as active, so its owner could never be deactivated.

31
00:01:43,936 --> 00:01:46,624
Nothing stopped an admin deactivating the last admin.

32
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:51,381
And digest items named only the client, which is ambiguous once a client has two projects.

33
00:01:51,701 --> 00:01:53,700
Every gap is now fixed in the spec.

34
00:01:54,783 --> 00:01:57,194
Passing every check isn't the same as production-ready.

35
00:01:57,386 --> 00:02:02,740
We didn't test production sign-in, real email delivery, the hourly job on a real scheduler, or hosting.

36
00:02:02,932 --> 00:02:05,145
Those are yours to check before your team relies on it.

37
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:07,444
So is it worth building?

38
00:02:07,743 --> 00:02:13,908
Rocketlane's Standard plan is forty-nine dollars per team member per month, billed annually, with a minimum of five.

39
00:02:14,143 --> 00:02:16,895
That's at least two thousand nine hundred and forty dollars a year.

40
00:02:17,087 --> 00:02:18,561
Premium is sixty-nine.

41
00:02:18,798 --> 00:02:23,769
If clients need to log in and tick off their own tasks, or you bill for time, buy Rocketlane.

42
00:02:24,046 --> 00:02:27,118
If you run the same checklist for a few clients a month, build it.

43
00:02:28,217 --> 00:02:29,583
Everything is in one file.

44
00:02:29,796 --> 00:02:36,543
The data model, eighteen rules, seed data, four phase prompts, and all twenty-eight checks, with the eighteen fixes already in.

45
00:02:37,533 --> 00:02:41,106
The full spec is free at personal software factory dot com.

46
00:02:41,106 --> 00:02:43,286
If you build it, tell me what broke.

47
00:02:43,286 --> 00:02:43,771
I'm Sam.

48
00:02:43,771 --> 00:02:44,800
See you tomorrow.
